a) It is an atrocity of a word
b) What's "the free world"? According to the main British and American
authorities, the preferred spelling would be "utilize".  Canadian
authorities likewise prefer -ize.  Australia and NZ prefer -ise but
even there -ize is an acceptable variant.

-ize is the traditional British spelling, and is preferred by the
pedantic language authorities (e.g. the OED and Fowler's Modern
English Usage, and the Encyclopaedia Britannica long before it was US-
owned), the most respected British technical journals (e.g. Nature)
and was the preferred form in the most respected news media (e.g. the
Times of London) until the late 1980s.  In the US, it's the only
correct form.  It's also is the closest to the original Greek and
Latin roots.

The main places that -ise is used are recent 20th century corruptions,
mostly misinformed Commonwealthers who believe that anything spelled
differently from the American manner is by definition correct.  Even
today in England, the ratio of -ise to -ize is only about 3:2, so
there's hope that the correct form will once again prevail.

As the OED says, when the etymology and pronunciation both point to
one spelling and that's the common historical spelling, there's no
reason to go with a more recent French variant:

"[T]he suffix..., whatever the element to which it is added, is in its
origin the...Latin -izare; and, as the pronunciation is also with z,
there is no reason why in English the special French spelling in -iser
should be followed, in opposition to that which is at once
etymological and phonetic."


